From 8e3f370b3f19109457b38b899ee0a528e2a2006d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 21:25:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+ on i915 at
 2160p24, 25 and 30, with metadata in the commit of the mode

i915 and xe signal 2160p24, 25 and 30 (and 4096x2160p24) with the HDMI
VIC in the HDMI vendor InfoFrame and VIC 0 in the AVI InfoFrame. HDR10+
takes that vendor DIP, so with HDR10+ the mode goes out with its CTA VIC
in the AVI InfoFrame instead (linux-9982), which only a commit that may
change the mode can set: a fastset compares the AVI InfoFrame. A film's
own HDR10+ goes in the commit of the mode of the video with its first
frame (CHDR10PlusOutput::WithMode()). Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+ had
nothing there: the metadata of a picture are measured on the GPU once it
is drawn and read back a frame or more later. The mode went out with
its HDMI VIC, and every commit with HDR10+ after it was refused in its
test, silently in the kernel log (only a debug line), until the video
went on as HDR10 after 8 of them. 1.0 RC4 thus sent every Dolby Vision
video as HDR10 there (issue 2: an i3-1005G1 with a Samsung S90H at
2160p23.976; the same path worked on NVIDIA with a 1440p monitor).

Where the driver needs it (CDVPlusModeStart::NeededFor(): i915 or xe, at
those modes), the first picture of the video, drawn for the commit of
its mode, waits for its own measurement, two periods of the mode and
100 ms at most (a change of mode takes far longer at the display), and
goes out with it in that commit. Where it does not come by then, it
goes with provisional metadata from the level 1 block of its RPU
(dvbridge_dvplus_provisional()), which the following pictures keep until
the first measurement replaces them, at most 48 pictures; without a
level 1 block, as before. Elsewhere (amdgpu, nvidia-drm, i915 at other
modes, where HDR10+ starts with a flip) nothing changes. kodi.log says
which it was and how long the picture waited.

The warning after a driver refused 8 commits with HDR10+ now says the
likely reason where the commit of the mode went without metadata on
such a driver and mode, instead of only the error.
